1
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:07,360
Notice that besides RMW instructions 
and semaphores, there are more  

2
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:12,400
available synchronization mechanisms.
Linux for instance offers synchronization  

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:17,520
primitives like mutex, condition variables, 
monitors, and barriers, among others.

4
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:23,760
Which one to use is something that depends on the 
application. For instance, if you have to choose  

5
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:30,640
between an entry section that relies on spinning 
or one that uses a Linux futex (which tries to  

6
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:35,840
acquire a lock in the entry section but instead 
of spinning puts the thread in the waiting queue),  

7
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:41,920
you might want to consider how long the 
critical section is. It might be OK to  

8
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:48,560
spin and "waste" CPU for a relatively short 
critical section that happens sporadically,  

9
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:52,560
but maybe not for a long one 
that is executed frequently...

10
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:58,080
Windows too offers synchronization 
primitives, for instance interrupt  

11
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:04,240
masks to protect access to global resources 
on uniprocessors and busy-wait spinlocks for  

12
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:11,200
multiprocessors (we have discussed before why 
disabling interrupts on a single processor might  

13
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:17,520
be not enough to have valid solutions to the 
critical section problem). Finally, it also  

14
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:23,920
offers objects like mutexes, semaphores or to 
provide events (similar to condition variables).

15
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:31,760
If we look at pthreads, you will see it also 
offers synchronization mechanisms like mutex locks  

16
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:36,560
with blocking queues, which can also 
support fairness, condition variables,  

17
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:38,400
and other non-portable extensions.

18
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:46,160
Notice that an OS that defines and offers 
such synchronization primitives usually needs  

19
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:49,600
such synchronization primitives itself, to protect  

20
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:56,160
its shared variables when multiple 
concurrent/parallel threads run the OS code!

